# `/ its ascii` is the same arm against ITSELF on the ascii set, so it isolates
# what the content costs that arm with the arm held constant. R4's sharpened
# half says to prefer that form: this slice has already seen a within-arm delta
# reproduce across builds where the ratio to a third arm drifted.

================================================================================
 WHAT THE RUN ABOVE ESTABLISHES
================================================================================

1. DEFECT D20, FOUND ON THE FIRST RUN, AND IT IS NOT A CONTENT-SET DEFECT

   The pass reported P3.1 disagreeing on latin1 and wide and agreeing on ascii,
   which reads like a string-path defect.  It is not.  **ASCII reproduces it.**
   What the extension actually changed was the ORDER in which payloads share one
   encode context, and that is what exposed it:

     ASCII, M3 alone                : ffi=12,097   want=12,097   ok
     ASCII, M3 after an M5 encode   : ffi=1,388,437 want=12,097  DIFFER

   **An empty Rust string's data pointer IS `AK_STR_DIRECT`.**

     String::new().as_ptr() = 0x1
     "".as_ptr()            = 0x1
     AK_STR_DIRECT          = 0x1

   `<[u8]>::as_ptr()` on an empty slice returns the dangling-but-aligned address
   1, and ABI v1 section 8 reserves 1 as the sentinel for "these bytes are an
   argument of the call".  So **every empty string and every empty bytes field
   took the direct-argument path**, and `enc_blob` spliced in whatever
   `(*cx).direct` and `(*cx).direct_len` held.

   **Why it survived every gate this slice has.**  On a context that has never
   encoded a direct-argument message, `direct_len` is 0, so the direct path
   writes a zero-length field -- which is exactly what an empty field should be.
   **The wrong path produced the right bytes.**  Every arm, every payload, every
   content set, stage 1 through stage 5: all green.

   **What it takes to see it is a SEQUENCE**: encode `UploadResultDataMessage`
   on a context, then encode any other message on the SAME context.  Nothing in
   this slice did that.  Every case built its own value, and the M5 cases ran
   last in every loop.  This pass put them in the middle.

   **And the vector needs a PRESENT-BUT-EMPTY string, not an absent one.**  Of
   the three payloads now in the regression, only P3.1 catches it: P1.3 is the
   all-absent path (its strings are absent, `tc == null`, and never reach
   `enc_blob`) and P2.5 is half-absent.  P3.1 has 21 probes of 200 whose
   `opt_label` is present and zero-length, which is M3's explicit-presence shape.
   So R6's absent-path rule has a third case under it that nobody had separated:
   **absent, present-and-empty, present-and-non-empty**, and the middle one is
   the one no payload generator builds on purpose.

   FIXED in the host binding, which is where the defect is: the core behaves
   exactly as section 8 specifies, and it is the HOST that must not hand it a
   data pointer of 1 for a non-direct field.  `str_arg` and a new `blob_arg` now
   route through `data_of`, which emits a null data pointer for an empty slice.
   The ABI already distinguishes absent from empty by `tc` (null means absent,
   set with `len == 0` means empty), so `data` is free to be null and the core
   never dereferences a zero-length span.

   REGRESSION, and it has been seen failing: `conformance` now encodes M5 on a
   context and then re-encodes P3.1, P1.3 and P2.5 on that same context.  With
   the fix reverted the gate reports P3.1 at 1,388,437 against 12,097 and exits
   1; with the fix it exits 0.

   **THE PART THAT IS NOT THIS SLICE'S TO DECIDE.**  The fix above closes it for
   a Rust host.  The HAZARD is in the ABI: section 8 picks a sentinel from a
   range that a legal empty buffer can occupy, and nothing in the specification
   tells a binding author not to pass it.  A C++ host returning `std::string{}.data()`
   or a JVM host passing the base of a zero-length array can land anywhere,
   including on 1.  Raised for the aggregating session, not taken: the options
   (a sentinel outside any plausible address, a length check beside the pointer
   check, or a rule stated in section 8 for binding authors) are ABI decisions.

2. THE ABSENT-PATH CONTROLS FIRED, WHICH IS WHY THEY WERE INCLUDED

   P1.3 reads 1.00 bytes-vs-ascii on every set and 0.99 to 1.02 in time on every
   arm and both directions.  Its strings are absent, so the content set has
   nothing to act on, and a row that MOVED there would have meant the harness was
   constructing strings that should not exist.  P6.1 (packed scalars, few
   strings) is the same at 1.06 and 1.12 bytes and ~1.0 in time.
   P5.2 reads 1.00 bytes: a `bytes` field carries no content set at all, and only
   the small string fields beside the 64 KB blob move it (65,620 -> 65,692 -> 65,764).

3. DECODE SCALES ALL ARMS TOGETHER, ON THE WHOLE SET AND NOT JUST P1.2 AND P2.2

   `/ its ascii`, the within-arm form R4's sharpened half prefers:

     payload  arm              latin1   wide
     P1.2     prost             1.407  1.988
              core-native       1.330  1.915
              core-ffi-rust     1.396  1.880
     P2.4     prost             1.505  2.515
              core-native       1.458  2.083
              core-ffi-rust     1.644  2.384

   prost moves as much as the core arms or more, everywhere.  **So the earlier
   conclusion -- "the content set changes no decode verdict" -- holds across the
   whole payload set**, and it is now a statement about eleven payloads rather
   than two.

   What is NEW is the magnitude: the ratio to prost moves by up to 0.28 on a
   decode row (P1.1, 0.961 ascii to 0.704 wide).  The verdict does not flip;
   the margin does, and a report quoting a decode margin should say which set.

4. AND ON ENCODE THERE IS ONE VERDICT FLIP, ON THE PAYLOAD BUILT TO DEFEAT THE
   LEARNED WIDTH

     core-ffi-rust / prost, P2.4 encode:   ascii 1.120   latin1 0.964   wide 0.766

   On ASCII the core LOSES to prost by 12 percent; on wide it WINS by 23.  P2.4
   is the decision-5 payload -- it alternates 3 and 150 repeated strings per
   element so that a per-site learned width is wrong on every element -- and as
   the content widens, prost's own cost grows faster than the core's.  **This is
   the only sign change in the table** (every other row's spread is under 0.14,
   and most are under 0.06), and it was unreachable before this pass because
   P2.4 had only ever been run on ASCII.

   It does not overturn decision 5, which was answered by an in-process
   isolation against two size-matched uniform arms, not by this ratio.  What it
   says is narrower and worth carrying: **the payload built to defeat the
   mechanism is also the payload whose verdict is most content-dependent.**

5. WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH

   * **No manifest oracle for latin1 or wide.**  `ffi/schema/` emits ASCII only,
     so correctness here is the four arms against the prost arm, which is itself
     manifest-validated on ASCII.  A wrong byte common to all four would pass.
   * **Rust, so no transcoder.**  These rows price width and validation.  The
     narrowing cost that dominates a managed host is structurally absent.
   * **P5.3, P5.4 and P7.1 are not in the timing pass.**  The first two are
     multi-megabyte bulk bytes with no meaningful string content, and P7.1 is
     decode-only with no canonical writer.  P5.1 and P5.2 are in the correctness
     pass as the bytes control.
   * **One machine, one microarchitecture, AVX2 present**, and the SIMD validator
     is not an arm here at all (decision 3 removed validation from encode; the
     decode-side policy is `stage3-decode-utf8-policy.log`).
   * **D20's fix is the host binding's.**  Whether the ABI should also move the
     sentinel, add a length check, or state a rule for binding authors is not
     decided here, and the other four slices' bindings have not been checked for
     the same collision.
